{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1462492-v20-celerity-blood-usage","id":1462492,"title":"V20 Celerity Blood usage","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-10-02T17:02:31-07:00","id":1462492,"title":"V20 Celerity Blood usage","content":"Greetings,<br /> <br /> <br /> I am confused on the wording for extra actions with Celerity, do you need to spend a Blood Point <b>per</b> extra action, or does a single Blood Point grant you <b>up to</b> Celerity dots in extra actions?<br /> <br /> <br /> Thanks,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-02T17:26:11-07:00","id":1462493,"title":"","content":"One extra action per blood point spent, to a maximum of your Celerity rating.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-02T22:34:43-07:00","id":1462514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1462492-v20-celerity-blood-usage?p=1462493#post1462493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One extra action per blood point spent, to a maximum of your Celerity rating.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never liked that. Revised (3rd. Edition) was the best version. V20 Dark Ages...Close second...In some parts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2582-gangrel44","name":"Gangrel44","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-02T23:21:29-07:00","id":1462518,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2582-gangrel44\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2582_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2582\" data-vbnamecard=\"2582\">Gangrel44</a> why do you like the other versions better?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26925-denstoffy","name":"Denstoffy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26925_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-02T23:43:35-07:00","id":1462521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Denstoffy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1462492-v20-celerity-blood-usage?p=1462518#post1462518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2582-gangrel44\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2582_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2582\" data-vbnamecard=\"2582\">Gangrel44</a> why do you like the other versions better?</div> </div> </div> </div> Well, In revised (3rd Edition), the cost of activating Celerity was only a single blood point. In V20 Dark Ages, You could add your Celerity rating to your Initiative, and multiply your movement speed by 1+ your Celerity rating. Imagine if you could add those two versions together. You'd have one badass discipline.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2582-gangrel44","name":"Gangrel44","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-03T00:50:36-07:00","id":1462526,"title":"","content":"1 BP for multiple extra-actions was considered pretty damned broken.<br /> <br /> Though in V20, your Celerity rating when you don't take multiple actions adds to all Dexterity dice pools, and to your Initiative (confusingly that's clarified in the combat section, not the Celerity section). It doesn't passively increase your speed like it does in DAV20,<br /> <br /> DAV20's movement boost costs an additional 1 BP, and only increases how far you can move in a turn. You can only take one movement action. V20's version lets you use your extra actions for movement actions. The V20 version isn't as good in raw contest of speed, but has more utility since you can move/attack/move.<br /> <br /> It's also hard to compare because VtM Rev., V20, and DAV20 all have different multiple action rules for mundane multiple actions, which mean's they're not directly comparable.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-03T23:38:12-07:00","id":1462623,"title":"","content":"Yes, I thought on talking about the other versions at first, but didn't precisely because there was no point, really.<br /> <br /> Can't blame a player on liking Rev Celerity. Anyone would, it is the most brokenly overpowered Discipline in the game bar none. It isn't right to erroneously guide an ST in that direction or give false expectations to a player when V20's Celerity is a pretty good option as is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T04:21:36-07:00","id":1462642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1462492-v20-celerity-blood-usage?p=1462526#post1462526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's also hard to compare because VtM Rev., V20, and DAV20 all have different multiple action rules for mundane multiple actions, which mean's they're not directly comparable.</div> </div> </div> </div>THIS is the biggie.<br /> <br /> REV just gave a -1 die penalty per extra action taken in a turn. If you had big enough dice pools, five attacks at -5 dice per attacks would be entirely doable without Celerity. Rev Celerity was basically just +1 die per rank in it to the extra actions. That's powerful, but not broken in the way it would have been if still using the 1e, 2e or 20e multiple action rules.<br /> <br /> DAV20 does normal additional actions by putting a penalty of -1 die and +1 difficulty per action (if the difficulty reaches 10 you cannot do it). Each point of Celerity already passively deals with the -1 die penalty just by giving you more Dex and the single blood point just allows you to ignore Celerity rating's worth of difficulty penalties. In context its well balanced to the rest of the action system because even a normal person could take the same number of extra actions provided they had enough dice to do and so long as the difficulties of them stayed 9 or less.<br /> <br /> Normal V20 doesn't have any difficulty penalties (so the DAV20 Celerity is actually pretty worthless using V20 Multiple Actions), instead it splits your lowest dice pool between all the actions you're attempting. In this case, 1 BP basically gives you a whole extra turn (albeit unable to be split) with each action using their full dice pools and so requiring a blood point PER extra full action makes sense because its not negating difficulty penalties its adding potentially buckets of dice.<br /> <br /> Generally a more action focused campaign will capture more of that feel if you use the DAV combat rules (including its version of Celerity), while games where you want combat breaking out to be essentially a &quot;fail state&quot; will find the V20 rules (and its version of Celerity) a better fit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1328,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","user":"Chris24601","date":". 10-04-2021, 07:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T11:03:48-07:00","id":1462674,"title":"","content":"Even for combat-oriented games I wouldn't use any rules that make multiple actions easier. Action economy is the backbone of combat in any RPG and multiple actions are extremely effective at breaking it to shreds. Celerity in V20 makes it possible but costly, which is the best I can expect from the Discipline short of using Requiem's Celerity (that give you a host of other benefits and no extra actions). As an ST and GM for other games I've sawn the power of extra actions everywhere and I can say for sure that it is actually more problematic on combat focused games than non-combat games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T11:35:20-07:00","id":1462682,"title":"","content":"\u200b<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1328\" data-vbnamecard=\"1328\">Chris24601</a> Just for the sake of theory.<br /> <br /> Basically the best discipline for multiple actions is temporis followed by protean followed by celerity?<br /> Or am I reading something wrong? (and yes i'm aware i'm comparing level 9's, otherwise Protean doesn't even have multiple actions)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26925-denstoffy","name":"Denstoffy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26925_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T11:51:17-07:00","id":1462686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Denstoffy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1462492-v20-celerity-blood-usage?p=1462682#post1462682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200b<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1328\" data-vbnamecard=\"1328\">Chris24601</a> Just for the sake of theory.<br /> <br /> Basically the best discipline for multiple actions is temporis followed by protean followed by celerity?<br /> Or am I reading something wrong? (and yes i'm aware i'm comparing level 9's, otherwise Protean doesn't even have multiple actions)</div> </div> </div> </div>To answer that we need to know which version of the multiple action rules you're using... V20, DAV20 or Rev. The answer will be different depending on which system is in use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T12:37:03-07:00","id":1462693,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Denstoffy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1462492-v20-celerity-blood-usage?p=1462682#post1462682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200b<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1328\" data-vbnamecard=\"1328\">Chris24601</a> Just for the sake of theory.<br /> <br /> Basically the best discipline for multiple actions is temporis followed by protean followed by celerity?<br /> Or am I reading something wrong? (and yes i'm aware i'm comparing level 9's, otherwise Protean doesn't even have multiple actions)</div> </div> </div> </div>As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1328\" data-vbnamecard=\"1328\">Chris24601</a> said it depends on version, but assuming V20:<br /> <br /> Temporis explicitly forbids accumulating its multiple actions effects with any other Discipline. Level 5, <b>Clotho's Gift</b>, causes instant Final Death if you try to use any Discipline for extra actions, while <b>Cheat the Fates</b> robs you of <i>EVERY</i> discipline during its effects, even passive ones like Fortitude.<br /> <br /> Protean 9 <b>Inward Focus</b> and Celerity do work together, but you must pay attention that it will be a simple addition of extra actions. They don't multiply as they don't split actions, just add then to the end of the round and Celerity specifically forbids splitting extra actions. And <b>Inward Focus</b> explicitly states that it uses as base your unmodified Dex, so no Celerity Bonus will help you regardless of order or if you have <b>Flower of Death</b> or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T12:55:04-07:00","id":1462695,"title":"","content":"To expand on my answer;<br /> <br /> In DAV20 Temporis' multi-action power is Clotho's Gift which is rank 5, and takes 3 BP and a roll at diff 7 to gain Celerity of equal rank to your Temporis for 1 turn per success... so its only better than Celerity if you can consistently roll 3 successes or more.<br /> <br /> DAV Protean's only &quot;extra actions&quot; that I could find would be using the Shape of the Beast's Wrath form to add Dex, but you'd still have the difficulty penalties and need to spend 3 BP and 3 turns to activate it... plus you're a big scary monster for the duration.<br /> <br /> I would rank both behind Celerity because Celerity in DAV is 1 BP to gain its benefits for 1 turn... meaning if you only need one turn of Celerity you're only down a blood point while Temporis down 3 whether they needed to use all of it or not. High end Celerity also includes its own optional powers, but just as a baseline Celerity wins in the multi-action contest in DAV20.<br /> <br /> * * * *<br /> <br /> V20 Temporis is again 3 BP, but the duration is fixed (1 turn per two ranks in Temporis) and the number of extra actions per turn is equal to the successes rolled at diff 7. For a non-Elder that's pretty much two turns with extra actions equal to the check result... probably a net savings over Celerity with the trade off being you don't have full control over the outcome and can't shorten the duration either.<br /> <br /> V20 Protean is basically 4+2 BP per turn at rank 9... Yeah, its better than Celerity and can even be used in conjunction with it, but only 4th Gens and Golconda types are even going to have the power so basing &quot;best&quot; on that feels specious.<br /> <br /> V20 Celerity is +rank to Dex pools and 1 extra action per BP. Elder level also has some options, but yes, as far as the rank nine power and Temporis its probably the weakest of the three.<br /> <br /> * * * *<br /> <br /> I have to dig my Revised books out to even get the specifics on Temporis and Protean from them.<br /> <br /> So basically, if you're going DAV20 rules for multiple actions, Celerity is the definite winner... passive +Dex eats half the multi-action penalties all on its own (vs. not having Celerity) and its just 1 BP per turn to counter the other half up to your rating with complete control over its starting and ending without any dice needed.<br /> <br /> V20 I'd give the edge to Temporis since its probably going to be more blood efficient and doesn't require you to be a 4th Gen just to use it, with Celerity as a close second for the simple reason that its useful right out of the gate while Temporis really needs some ranks in it before the multi-action cheese starts flowing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T13:09:11-07:00","id":1462698,"title":"","content":"Yeah, to V20 I'd say Temporis only wins in the blood economy aspect. If you really want to run this competition to level 9 then Protean IS better, full stop, but that's a lot to wait for multi-actions. Temporis and Celerity kind of draw, as one is more cost-effective while the other will give you extra actions more consistently from the get go. Celerity is better than Temporis in sheer power, hands down, if a few bursts of action are all you need, and this is frequently the case.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}